{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target proposes an 'integration' relation but specifies no causal mechanism connecting checklist complexity to expert reliability. It asserts a dependence ('reliability of the expert depends on the absence of blind-ticking') without explaining HOW complexity produces blind-ticking or how blind-ticking degrades verification quality. The 'refuted if' clause is epistemic scaffolding (a falsifiability condition), not a mechanism. A substantive integration relation must identify the operative causal chain; here the causal link between the endpoints is merely asserted, not proposed.","counterexample":["A target stating: 'Checklist complexity increases blind-ticking via attention depletion: as item count rises, experts shift from item-level verification to pattern-completion, ticking items they did not independently assess. This degrades reliability because unchecked items propagate defects. The relation is refuted if defect-detection rates remain constant across complexity levels.' This names the mechanism (attention depletion → pattern-completion) linking the endpoints."],"target":"3e0f22c13b3e839ea3e90423bc994e6a427b69f449327db148da0d627ab22885"}]}